Most common problems 


Your Network ACL has a problem ❌

        You currently have:

        Rule    Action

        100 Allow All Traffic   ✅

        * Deny All Traffic      ❌ default

   The * Deny is normal, BUT the issue is probably your inbound/outbound ephemeral ports are not properly allowed.

   For AWS NACLs, you must allow:

  outbound HTTP/HTTPS
  return traffic (ephemeral ports)

Why This Happens

APT uses:

        HTTPS port 443 outbound
        Random ephemeral return ports

        Without ephemeral ports:

        connection starts
        response packets blocked
        apt hangs forever

Ephemeral:

  Ephemeral ports are temporary/random ports used by your server for outgoing connections.

  When your EC2 instance connects to the internet (APT, curl, wget, browser requests, APIs), it does not use only port 80/443.

Instead:

 Destination server uses:
 HTTP → port 80
 HTTPS → port 443

 Your EC2 uses a random temporary source port like:
  32768
  49152
  55001
   etc.

 These random ports are called ephemeral ports.


 Example: 


        Your EC2 runs:

                sudo apt update

        Connection becomes:

        EC2-IP:54021  --->  archive.ubuntu.com:443

        Here:

                443 = destination HTTPS port
        
                54021 = temporary ephemeral port

        The Ubuntu server sends response back to:

                archive.ubuntu.com:443 ---> EC2-IP:54021

                If NACL blocks port 54021,
           then:

           request goes out ✅

          response blocked ❌
        
        apt hangs forever

-------------------------------------------------------

Why Security Groups Usually Work Automatically:

        Amazon Web Services Security Groups are stateful:

  request allowed out
 
 response automatically allowed back

 So you normally don't worry about ephemeral ports there.

----------------------------------------------------

Why NACL Needs Ephemeral Ports

Network ACLs are stateless:

    they do NOT remember connections
    every response packet must be explicitly allowed

Therefore AWS requires:

   outbound 80/443
   inbound ephemeral ports
   sometimes outbound ephemeral too

------------------------------------------------------

Common Ephemeral Port Range

  Linux usually uses:

     1024-65535

AWS recommends allowing:

     TCP 1024-65535


---------------------------------------------------


Real-World Services Using Ephemeral Ports

These all need ephemeral ports:

apt update
curl
wget
APIs
Docker pull
Kubernetes nodes
Database clients
Browsers
SSH responses

--------------------------------------------


Stateful vs Stateless

Feature                 Security Group          NACL

Type                    Stateful                Stateless
Remembers connection    ✅                       ❌
Need ephemeral rules    ❌ Usually No            ✅ Yes
Applied at              ENI/Instance             Subnet


